hexagon: describe the conversion word of the int32 accumulator read
Fold-into: hexagon-hmx-i8/0001-hexagon-hmx-int8-macros.patch

The comment of the int8 section of hmx-utils.h told that HMX_I32_READ_SCALES
holds four f16 scales (2^9, 2^1, 2^-7 and 2^-15). A probe of each field of
the word in the simulator, and the same probe streams on the v79 silicon,
show a different format. Each value is a conversion word. Bits 0..9 are a
mantissa m, bits 10..14 are an exponent e, and the scale is
(1 + m / 1024) * 2^(e - 24). Bit 15 is the sign, bits 16..18 select an
activation function, and bits 19..30 are a u8 bias with 4 fraction bits.
The four words have e = 24, 16, 8 and 0, thus they read byte 0, 1, 2 and 3
of the accumulator. The constants are correct, and only the comment changes.

The comment also gives these facts from the same probe:
- The u16 stores (acc:2x1 and acc:2x2) write the maximum of two rows or of
  2x2 elements, thus no store gives the full int32 value.
- mxclracc and a store without :retain clear the accumulator. After
  mxswapacc, they clear the two accumulators.

The header of 0001 gives approximately 711 processor cycles for one int32
read. That number includes the call overhead of the vendor library. On the
v79 silicon, one four-plane read of a 64 by 32 tile in an inline asm chain
costs 55 to 60 processor cycles.

The patch changes comments only. Two builds of libggml-htp-v79.so from the
pinned commit plus the series, without and with this patch, give the same
bytes (sha256 1bb393c868c36736c11991835019833b1eac14fc67f69bdee73d288c76ebfb10).
